dwm - dynamic window manager
============================
dwm is an extremely fast, small, and dynamic window manager for X.


Requirements
------------
In order to build dwm you need Xlib, Xft, Fontconfig, Xinerama,
XComposite, XDamage and XRender development headers and libraries.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dwm is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dwm (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dwm
-----------
Add the following line to your .xinitrc to start dwm using startx:

    exec dwm

In order to connect dwm to a specific display, make sure that
the DISPLAY environment variable is set correctly, e.g.:

    DISPLAY=foo.bar:1 exec dwm

(This will start dwm on display :1 of the host foo.bar.)

In order to display status info in the bar, you can do something
like this in your .xinitrc:

    while xsetroot -name "`date` `uptime | sed 's/.*,//'`"
    do
    	sleep 1
    done &
    exec dwm


Configuration
-------------
The configuration of dwm is done by creating a custom config.h
and (re)compiling the source code.

Window switcher
---------------
Alt+Tab opens a fast, most-recently-used list of windows on the current
monitor, including windows on other tags. Keep Alt held and press Tab (or
Shift+Tab) to cycle; release Alt or press Enter to select. Escape cancels.
Press Delete to close the highlighted window without switching to its tag; the
switcher stays open. Apps can prompt to save changes after you release Alt.
As with Alt+C, apps without WM_DELETE_WINDOW support are forcibly closed.
While the switcher is open, press 1–9 to preview/filter that tag's windows,
or 0 to show all tags. Selecting a hidden window switches to its tag. The
popup shows a live preview of the selected window using XComposite, XDamage
and XRender, alongside window titles. It uses the Quickshell palette and
monospace font. If the X server lacks those extensions, titles still work. Alt+Ctrl+Tab retains the previous-tag shortcut.
Rebuild and restart dwm after changing the configuration.

Monitor switcher
----------------
With at least two monitors, hold Alt and press ` (grave) to open the monitor
switcher. It previews the windows on each monitor's currently visible tags in
a miniature layout and lists their titles. Press `, Tab or the arrow keys to
choose a display; Shift reverses cycling. Release Alt or press Enter to focus
the selected monitor and move the pointer there; Escape cancels. For more
than three monitors, the cards page automatically as you cycle. The previews
use the same X extensions and fallback as the window switcher. Existing
Alt+comma and Alt+period monitor focus shortcuts still work.

Waybar-inspired dwm bar
-----------------------
The old dwm status bar is replaced with a built-in, top-positioned bar. It
uses the Waybar Editor Dark palette, JetBrainsMono Nerd Font, rounded pills,
centered clickable tags, occupied/urgent indicators, and a blue bottom line.
The bar is enabled by default on every monitor; Alt+B toggles it per monitor.
The XEmbed system tray follows the focused monitor. Tags keep their usual
left/right click and Alt+click actions.

The bar starts ~/.config/dwm/bar-status.sh automatically (or
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dwm/bar-status.sh if XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set). Copy this file
along with the rest of config/dwm, then rebuild and restart dwm. The helper
updates CPU usage, default audio sink, network and local date/time every two
seconds; it exits after dwm exits. It uses the X root window name, replacing
external xsetroot status writers and the old status2d text format. If the
helper or a dependency is missing, unavailable readings show placeholders.

- Audio: left or right click toggles mute, scroll changes volume by 2%, and
  middle click opens pavucontrol. Requires pactl and a PulseAudio-compatible
  server (PulseAudio or PipeWire's pulse server); pavucontrol is optional.
- Network: left or right click opens nm-connection-editor. NetworkManager's
  nmcli provides the connection type; without it, a basic link-state fallback
  is used. The editor is optional.
- CPU: left click opens top in Ghostty. Clock: left click opens gsimplecal.
  Ghostty and gsimplecal are optional; date and time are always displayed.
- xsetroot, a POSIX shell, date, sed and /proc/stat are used by the helper.
  A Nerd Font is needed for the icons. On narrow monitors, modules are hidden
  as needed to prioritize tags and the clock.
